<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prussia, Prussian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Prussia was a German state ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern dynasty</a> from 1701, known for its efficient bureaucracy and powerful army. It gained territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and in the partitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, and rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. After victories over Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Prussia led the unification that founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Prussia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawing created by David Liuzzo · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_(1803-1892).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rivalry with <b>Prussia</b> over leadership in Germany came to a head after the joint war against Denmark in 1864.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Conrad and Frederick at first held the inheritance together and then divided it, probably by about 1218: Conrad took the burgraviate of Nuremberg and founded the Franconian line, which later ruled Brandenburg, <b>Prussia</b> and the German Empire, while Frederick kept the ancestral Swabian lands as Count of Zollern.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…fed the Patriot movement, which demanded reform and attacked the powers of the stadtholder William V. When the Patriots gained control of several towns and provinces, <b>Prussia</b> invaded in 1787 to restore William V, whose wife Wilhelmina was the Prussian king’s sister, and many Patriots fled to France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Silesian Wars Saxony was defeated by <b>Prussia</b> at Kesselsdorf in 1745, and in 1756 Frederick the Great invaded without warning; the Saxon army capitulated at Pirna and was forced into Prussian service, and the country remained occupied for most of the Seven Years’ War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Nazi regime its self-government was subordinated to a Reich governor, and the Greater Hamburg Act of 1937 joined it with the Prussian towns of Altona, Wandsbek and Harburg-Wilhelmsburg while transferring Cuxhaven to <b>Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>…Act of the Congress of Vienna, joined 39 sovereign states and free cities: the Austrian Empire and the <b>Kingdom of Prussia</b> for their former imperial lands, kingdoms such as Bavaria, Saxony, Württemberg and Hanover, dozens of grand duchies, duchies and principalities, and the free cities of Frankfurt, Hamburg,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
